+# AGENTS.md
+
+Repository-wide instructions for AI agents and automation working on **Pox Kernel for Redmi Note 8 Pro (begonia / MT6785)**.
+
+This file applies to the whole repository unless a nested `AGENTS.md` overrides it. Read it before making changes.
+
+## 1. Read first
+
+- `BRANCHES.md` — branch/tag architecture, release matrix, build mapping.
+- `CONTRIBUTING.md` — feature, integration, and release rules.
+- `CONFLICTS.md` — migration and integration conflict log.
+- `README.md` — minimal project/build entry point.
+
+## 2. Repository facts
+
+- Device: Xiaomi Redmi Note 8 Pro (`begonia` / `begonia_in`), MediaTek MT6785 / Helio G90T.
+- Kernel: Linux 4.14.357, arm64/AArch64.
+- Toolchain: TheRagingBeast (TRB) Clang 18, bootstrapped by `kerdevdep/setup_kerdevdep.sh`.
+- Packaging: AnyKernel3; flashable zips are written to `build/`.
+- Single long-lived base: `common/base` (code-base tag `base-v1`).
+- Features: `feature/ksun`, `feature/bakasu`, `feature/resukisu`, `feature/apatch`, `feature/miui`, `feature/c2`.
+- Releases: `release/<combo>-YYYY-MM-DD` immutable tags.
+- Archives: `archive/<name>` tags for old branch heads.
+
+## 3. Branch model — hard rules
+
+- Work from `common/base`; keep `common/base` and `feature/*` as the working refs.
+- One feature per `feature/*` branch. Do not mix root, MIUI, and C2 changes in one feature.
+- Root features own their SUSFS/NoMount bundle. Do not split SUSFS or NoMount into separate features.
+- APatch is standalone and must not receive KernelSU/SUSFS content.
+- C2-only Saikrishna patches belong only in `feature/c2`.
+- MIUI/HyperOS compatibility is WLAN-focused and must not weaken base security or performance settings.
+- Do not create long-lived `dev`, `integration`, or `test` branches.
+  - Integration branches are `test/*`, ephemeral, and must be deleted after a release tag exists.
+- Never force-push `common/base`, `release/*`, or `archive/*`.
+- Archive a branch as `archive/<name>` before deleting it. Delete old branches only with maintainer approval.
+- Keep `onyx-kaeru` unless the maintainer explicitly approves removal.
+- Do not create `feature/perf`: `common/base` already contains the WALT, crypto, BFQ, zram writeback/zstd, xswapd, and gaming performance stack.
+
+## 4. Build and release rules
+
+- Never edit, create, delete, rebase, checkout, or stage files while a build, flash, or test process is running.
+- `build.sh` auto-bootstraps `kerdevdep` on first use. Do not mutate files during that process.
+- `build.sh` selects its variant from `GIT_BRANCH`. For a detached tag, set it explicitly:
+
+```sh
+# base
+bash build.sh all
+
+# feature branch
+GIT_BRANCH=feature/ksun bash build.sh all
+
+# release tag
+GIT_BRANCH=$(git describe --tags --exact-match) bash build.sh all
+
+# explicit override
+DEFCONFIG=begonia_ksu_next_defconfig VERSION_NAME="Onyx-KSU-Next" GIT_BRANCH=feature/ksun bash build.sh all
+```
+
+- Use `build.sh all` for kernel + AnyKernel3 zip. Other actions: `kernel`, `zip`, `install`, `build-install`, `menuconfig`, `clean`, `distclean`.
+- Release tags are immutable snapshots. Never rewrite or force-push them.
+- Do not push any branch or tag until verification is complete and the maintainer approves.
+
+## 5. Verification gates
+
+Before reporting success, run and record:
+
+```sh
+bash -n build.sh
+git grep -n -E '^(<<<<<<<|>>>>>>>) ' HEAD -- .
+git diff --check base-v1..HEAD
+```
+
+Check that the selected defconfig retains the base keys and the variant keys:
+
+```sh
+git show HEAD:arch/arm64/configs/<defconfig> | grep -E \
+  'CONFIG_(SCHED_WALT|CRYPTO_AES_ARM64|IOSCHED_BFQ|ZRAM|ZRAM_WRITEBACK|CRYPTO_ZSTD|KSU|KSU_SUSFS|NOMOUNT|KALLSYMS_ALL)='
+```
+
+Rules:
+
+- `git diff --check` may report pre-existing trailing whitespace in imported root/NoMount code. That is not a conflict, but it must be documented, not hidden.
+- A full kernel build is required before claiming build success. If only static checks were run, say exactly that.
+- Never invent or infer CI, build, flash, or runtime results.
+
+## 6. Kernel quality rules
+
+- Follow upstream Linux kernel coding style. Use 8-space tabs, not spaces.
+- Make the smallest sound, targeted diff. Do not reformat or touch unrelated files.
+- Never mark a sysctl or parameter as `const` if a vendor HAL or userspace writes it. Example: `sysctl_sched_migration_cost` must remain writable, otherwise Xiaomi Power HAL writes can trigger an EL1 Data Abort panic.
+- Enforce the USB 2.0 SDP 500 mA limit on standard PC USB hosts. High current (3.0 A / 18 W) is for AC/DCP chargers only.
+- Keep `UTS_RELEASE` within the kernel's 64-character limit. Localversion strings must be clamped.
+- Keep base performance settings intact: WALT, ARMv8 crypto, BFQ tuning, zram writeback/zstd, xswapd, gaming/dynamic-fsync.
+- Leave no debug printk floods, scratch scripts, ad-hoc binaries, or broken stubs.
+
+## 7. Commit and PR rules
+
+- Use Conventional Commits: `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>`.
+- Valid types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `chore`, `docs`, `ci`, `build`.
+- One logical change per commit. Avoid monolithic commits that mix drivers, configs, and CI.
+- Forbidden subjects: `fix`, `wip`, `update`, `temp`, `test`, `patch`.
+- Every non-trivial commit body must explain **why** the change is made.
+- Keep feature-branch history linear. Do not merge `common/base` into a feature branch.
+- Integration merges are only for ephemeral `test/*` branches and should use `git merge --no-ff`.
+- Log non-trivial conflict resolutions in `CONFLICTS.md`; never silently choose one side.
+- Tag a release only after the verification gates pass.
+
+PR checklist:
+
+1. [ ] Full kernel build passes with the repo toolchain.
+2. [ ] AnyKernel3 zip is produced and valid.
+3. [ ] `UTS_RELEASE` is ≤ 64 characters.
+4. [ ] Correct `feature/*` or release combination is targeted.
+5. [ ] Conventional Commits, linear history, no WIP or merge clutter.
+6. [ ] No charging, boot-stability, or HAL-sysctl regressions.
+7. [ ] Verification results are stated honestly; static-only results are labeled as such.
+
+## 8. Agent safety
+
+- Inspect existing files and user work before overwriting or deleting anything.
+- Do not delete old branches, rewrite shared history, or force-push without maintainer approval.
+- Do not push until the maintainer explicitly asks.
+- Archive before deletion and preserve `onyx-kaeru`.
+- Use `CONFLICTS.md` for any deliberate conflict resolution.
+- Prefer dedicated tools over destructive shell operations.
